On Sun, 22 Sep 2002, Russell Coker wrote:

> avc:  denied  { sigchld } for  pid=30758 exe=/usr/sbin/sshd
> scontext=root:user_r:newrole_t tcontext=system_u:system_r:sshd_t
> tclass=process
>
> I ssh'd into a machine and ran "exec newrole -r sysadm_r" to do some
> administration work.
>
> Then when I finished that I typed "exit" to end my shell, and the ssh
> connection was not closed as newrole_t did not have permission to send
> sigchld to sshd_t (I had to use "~.").
>
> The same avc message can be generated by using newrole to change role/domain
> and then doing "exec gpg" or "exec netscape", however newrole is sleeping on
> wait() so it doesn't cause any operational issues.

Naturally, this wouldn't be a problem if you ran newrole (or gpg or
netscape) in a child process of the shell rather than using 'exec' to
replace the shell.  In that case, the existing rules would permit sigchld
as needed.  Is such usage of 'exec' common?

> Hmm, how dangerous is sending sigchld to crond_t or other similar programs
> anyway?  Forcing netscape to do whatever I want is trivial, so this would
> allow me as user_t to send sigchld to any of those programs.

The default action for SIGCHLD is to ignore the signal, and I would expect
that explicit handlers for SIGCHLD would be relatively benign even when a
spurious SIGCHLD is received.  So it is likely not a big deal to grant
sigchld permission.

